    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE inhibitors and ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) are equally recommended as top-tier, first-line medications for high blood pressure .",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "ACE inhibitors",
            "link": "https://www.kidney.org/kidney-topics/ace-inhibitors-and-arbs"
          },
          {
            "text": "first-line medications for high blood pressure",
            "link": "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/HYPERTENSIONAHA.120.16667"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both ACE inhibitors and ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) are equally recommended as top-tier, first-line medications for high blood pressure"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "How They Work"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE inhibitors (names ending in \"-pril\", like lisinopril) stop your body from making a hormone called angiotensin II, which narrows blood vessels."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "(names ending in \"-sartan\", like losartan) block the receptor sites where angiotensin II attaches, keeping blood vessels relaxed."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Both lower blood pressure effectively and protect the heart and kidneys."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Differences"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects: ARBs are safer and better tolerated. ACE inhibitors frequently cause a persistent dry cough and carry a higher risk of angioedema (swelling of the lips or tongue) because they increase bradykinin levels. ARBs do not affect bradykinin, avoiding the dry cough."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Effectiveness: Large studies show both classes lower blood pressure and reduce heart attack or stroke risk equally well."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Cost: ACE inhibitors are usually generic and less expensive than ARBs."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Clinical Preference"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Recent large-scale data and guidelines increasingly favor prescribing ARBs over ACE inhibitors initially because patients are much less likely to stop taking ARBs due to side effects. Neither class should be used during pregnancy."
      }
    ],
